        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{3x}{y}+\\frac{9}{y^2}$, $y\\ne0$.\n\nUse the perfect-square identity, including twice the product of the two terms.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{y}\\right)^2&=\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2+2\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{y}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{y}\\right)^2\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{y}\\right)^2&=\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{3x}{y}+\\frac{9}{y^2}\\end{align*}$$\n\n$y\\ne0$, since the original expression contains a denominator $y$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
